y its reasons would have justified keeping every module. `tests/test_baseline_honesty.py` now carries two explicit sets over the workflow family alone — `WORKFLOW_SUBJECT_READERS` (7, each with a reason naming the read) and `WORKFLOW_INPUT_READERS_AWAITING_CONVERSION` (17) — asserted equal in both directions to what the scan reports a workflow read for, with an assertion that the three converted modules are no longer in it. `test_stage_output_ownership.py`'s reason no longer claims its workflow read is a subject.\n\n## What I did not finish\n\n**17 modules remain input readers**, named in `WORKFLOW_INPUT_READERS_AWAITING_CONVERSION`. The plan assigns all of them to this stage and I converted three of ~20; each is a 500–1900-line module needing its runner, its target and its harness fixture rewritten alongside the definition, and I ran out of turn before them. `test_stage_output_ownership.py` — the third module the verifier named — is among them; I read it through and stopped rather than half-convert it, since it writes `\"implementer\"`, `\"tester\"` and `\"tests/\"` inline in ~100 places and a partial pass would have left assertions pointing at two different workflows. The remaining work is now visible as a failing-if-stale declaration rather than as prose, so each future conversion has to move a name.","ttft_ms":2396,"ttft_stream_ms":1004,"time_to_request_ms":27,"type":"result","duration_ms":1717279,"uuid":"5e1e7407-3028-4c45-8f43-253bbc30ef6d"}
